Grade 1 radiocarpal joint sprain from bracing a fall to wrist
Minor Trauma and Personal Injury
Diffuse radiocarpal joint pain, FOOSH mechanism, Negative anatomical snuffbox tenderness with normal x-rays

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: MTI-009
Reason for Visit: Acute wrist pain following a mechanical fall, requiring assessment to exclude scaphoid or distal radius fracture.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage identified an acute wrist injury resulting from a fall onto an outstretched hand (FOOSH). Urgent evaluation was necessary to exclude structural fractures, particularly of the scaphoid, and prevent potential avascular necrosis or displacement before daytime imaging could be coordinated.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: I tripped and put my hand out to catch myself, and now my wrist aches badly when I move it.
History of Present Illness: Patient describes a fall onto an outstretched hand resulting in forceful dorsiflexion of the wrist. This was followed by mild, diffuse aching pain across the dorsal and volar aspects of the radiocarpal joint. The patient notes mild localized soft tissue swelling but no visible structural deformity or angulation. Pain is exacerbated by extreme ranges of wrist flexion and extension, both active and passive. The patient has preserved the ability to perform a pain-free, strong power grip.
Functional Status: Independent, but utilizing the uninjured hand for complex fine motor tasks.
Social/Safety Concerns: Minor hindrance in executing bilateral manual tasks.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 125/80 | HR: 78 | Temp: 36.7 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 70 Kg
General: Alert, holding wrist in a neutral, supported position.
Physical Exam: Upper limb examination reveals diffuse radiocarpal joint pain and mild localized soft tissue swelling. There is complete absence of focal bony point tenderness in the anatomical snuffbox. No point tenderness is elicited over the distal radius or ulnar styloid. Active and passive range of motion are full but provoke pain at extreme limits. Neuromuscular testing shows a normal, strong power grip and negative scaphoid tubercle tenderness with a pain-free axial load of the thumb metacarpal. Normal neurovascular status in the hand with intact sensation, brisk capillary refill, and normal motor function of all fingers.
Environmental Observations: Loose rug identified at the location of the fall; hazard mitigated.
Medication Reconciliation: Nil routine medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Grade 1 radiocarpal joint sprain - Status: Stable
Differentials:

Scaphoid fracture - Ruled out due to: Complete absence of anatomical snuffbox tenderness, scaphoid tubercle tenderness, and pain on axial loading of the thumb.

Distal radius fracture (Colles) - Ruled out due to: Intact bone contour, absence of severe focal radial point tenderness, and preserved strong grip strength.

Scapholunate dissociation - Ruled out due to: Absence of localized tenderness specifically over the scapholunate interval and no gross carpal instability on movement.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The FOOSH mechanism carries a high probability of occult carpal or distal radial fractures. Meticulous physical examination safely excluded scaphoid involvement and distal radius fractures through negative provocative testing and absence of bony tenderness. Urgent after-hours assessment was clinically indicated to rule out structural wrist injuries that would require immediate rigid cast immobilization, allowing safe provision of conservative management and avoiding unnecessary emergency department presentation overnight.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Advised standard supportive care. Standard posterior-anterior and lateral wrist radiographs are not acutely required based on clinical findings but are recommended if pain persists beyond 5 days.
Positive Behavioral Change: Utilize a removable wrist splint for comfort during activities, but deliberately remove it hourly to perform gentle range-of-motion exercises. This prevents severe joint stiffness while providing symptomatic relief.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): Removable velcro wrist splint.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 5-7 days if symptoms fail to resolve.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Present to the Emergency Department or local urgent care if the fingers become cold, pale, or numb, or if severe pain suddenly develops.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Occult scaphoid fractures can occasionally present with delayed bony tenderness; dynamic clinical monitoring is required.
Clinical Course: Mild aching typically resolves over 1-2 weeks. Severe loading or push-ups should be avoided for 4 weeks.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek further medical care if you develop localized severe pain at the base of your thumb over the next two days, or if your grip strength dramatically weakens.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: A wrist splint provides mechanical offloading to the inflamed joint capsule, reducing nociceptive pain signals during movement.
Actionable Steps: Wear the splint for heavy tasks. Remove the splint frequently to draw small circles with your hand. Avoid putting body weight through your hands.

